  "notes": "\"The point where trap-soul rejoined pop radio\", and harmonically the entry is explicit: \"4-chord cycles that actually cadence — `i–iv–VII–III` and `i–VI–III–VII` families — plus 90s-R&B borrowings and a modulating final chorus\". Those two families are authored verbatim and are the model's top weights; `maxChords` is 4 and `harmonicRhythm` moves to one chord per bar, which is what separates her from the 2-chord bench either side of her. The kit is the first of her two documented variants: \"a sparse West-Coast-club-adjacent kit with a snapping clap on beat 3, a single kick on 1, minimal hats and a bounce triplet feel\" — so `kick.densityPerBar` is [1,2], the hats are thin, and the swing is pushed to the triplet end of the legal band at 0.6. \"Finger-snaps and a hand-percussion layer are constants\" is the perc block. Her structural marker is authored as written: \"a genuine pre-chorus (rare in this lane) is one of her structural markers\". ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are the swing amount, every drum probability, the 808 numbers and the melody's density band — \"warm mid-alto, moderate note-density, gospel-inflected runs at phrase ends\" is a direction, and 4–8 notes per bar is this author's reading of it. ⚠ `confidence` is medium, mirroring the entry, whose tempo values are folded. ⚠ `session.bpm.mode` is **INFERRED**: the number is not stated in the entry, and it is an authored centre of gravity inside the band the entry does state. ⚠ **`session.keys` is INFERRED.** The entry's Keys line describes the harmonic grammar but names no key centre, so these values and their weights are an authored choice — no key in this set is stated in the research.",
